DILG Republic of the Philippines DEPARTMENT OF THE INTERIOR AND LOCAL GOVERNMENT DILG-NAPOLCOM Building, EDSA corner Quezon Avenue, Quezon City v,ww.dilg.gov.ph OFFICE OF THE UNDERSECRETARY FOR LOCAL GOVERNMENT DILG Legal opinion No. 01 S. 2016 JAN 1 1 2016 HON. PAMELA M. PARDO Acting Vice-Governor Province of Camarines Norte Dear Acting Vice-Governor Pardo, This refers to your 04 November 2015 letter requesting this Department's opinion on matters concerning the powers and functions of an Acting Vice-Governor, specifically as follows: I. 2. 3. 4. Can I, as Acting Vice-Governor, legally, lawfully or validly renew and/or replace the present casual/job order employees hired by Hon. Jonah Pedro Pimentel under the Office of the Vice-Governor? In case I am allowed to renew and/or replace casual/job order employees, when should the duration of the employment of my appointees last? Would such duration be until October 27, 2016, the very day when the suspension meted out by the Ombudsman upon Governor Tallado expires? In the contract of employment of those casual/job order employees, should the Governor of the Province be a party thereto, or should he have any participation in terms of the execution and approval thereof? In case the Governor must have no participation therein, should it be the Vice- Governor that must be a party therein or that must have participation therein, he/she being the head of the Sangguniang Panlalawigan? Considering that your queries are inter-related, we deem it appropriate to consolidate our answers thereto as follows: Applying the case of Menzon vs. Petilla (G.R. NO. 90762. May 20, 1991), which analogously applied the mode of succession for permanent vacancy in the Office of the Vice-Governor [there being no provision of law governing the mode of succession in case of a temporary vacancy in said office], the Acting Vice-Governor is authorized by law to automatically exercise the powers and perform the duties of the Vice-Governor.

I-I,owever, the power to preside over the sessions of tho sanggunian is not automatically given to the Acting Vice-Governor in view of the Supreme Court's ruling in Gamboa vs. Aguirre (G.R. No. 134213. July 20, 1999) stating that the issue of Presiding Officership in this kind of situation shall be resolved by applying Section 49 (b) Of the Local Government Code (hereinafter, the "Code"), in which case, the majority of those members present, there being a quorum, shall elect from among themselves the temporary Presiding Officer for that session. As such, the limitation provided under Section 46 (a) of the Code [on the power of the governor to appoint, hire or dismiss employees, unless the temporary vacancy in said office exceeds thirty (3()) days], in relation to Section 466 (a) (2)1 of the Local Government Code (hereinafter, the "Code") may be analogously applied in case of temporary vacancy in the Office of the Vice- Governor. It should be emphasized that as a necessary consequence of the Vice-Governor's exercise of the powers and functions of the Office of the Governor in an acting capacity, the elected Vice-Governor is legally deprived of exercising the functions appurtenant to his office by reason of the principle of separation of powers. Clearly, all powers and functions of the Office of the Vice-Governor are to be exercised by the Acting Vice-Governor, subject to certain limitations as above-discussed. Anent the renewal and/or replacement of job orders of the Sangguniang Panlalawigan (SP), the same is exercised by the Vice-Governor in relation to the latter's power to sign all warrants drawn on the provincial treasury for all expenditures appropriated for the operation of the SP pursuant to Section 466 (a) (1)2 of the Code, following the ruling of the Supreme Court in Atienza vs. Villarosa, etal. (G.R. No. 161081, IO May 2005), viz: "Reliance by the CA on the clause approval of the disbursement voucher by the local chief executive himself shall be required whenever local funds are disbursed Of the above section (Section 344) to rule that it is the Governor (Pho has the authority to approve purchase orders for the supplies, materials or equipment for the operation of the Sangguniang Panlalawigan is misplaced. This clause cannot prevail over the more specific clause of the same provision Il'hich provides that vouchers and payrolls shall be certified [o and approved by the head of the department or office who has administrative control of [he fund concerned. The Vice-Governor, as the presiding officer of the Sangguniang Panlalawigan, has administrative control of the funds of the said body. Accordingly, it is the Vice-Governor who has the authority to approve disbursement vouchers for expenditures appropriated for the operation of the Sangguniang Panlalawigan.' [Emphasis and underscoring supplied] Section 466. Powers, Duties, and Compensation. (a) The vice-governor shall: (2) Subject to civil service laws, rules and regulations, appoint all officials and employees of the Sangguniang panlalawigan, except those whose manner of appointment is specifically provided in this Code; Section 466. powers, Duties, and Compensation.- (a) The vice-governor shall: (1) Be the presiding officer of the sangguniang panlalawigan and sign all warrants drawn drawn on the provincial treasury for all expenditures appropriated for the operation of the Sangguniang panlalawigan;

Thé same may be applied in the exercise of powers and functions by the Acting Vice- Governor. As such, the latter is authorized to renew and/or replace job orders of the SP subject to certain limitations. Notably, Contract of Services/Job Orders are not considered government service pursuant to Section l, Rule Xl of Civil Service Commission (CSC) Memorandum Circular (MC) No. 40, s. 1998 and are not covered by Civil Service Rules and Regulations, but covered by Commission on Audit (COA) rules as described under Section 2 (c) thereof. Hence, it is suggested that reference to COA rules/guidelines on Contract of Services/Job Orders be made anent the limitations and conditions on the engagement of Job Orders. We hope to have enlightened you on the foregoing. Very truly yours, AUSTERE A. PANADERO Undersecretary DILG - OUSLG 2016-01-08-00077 s •'-7"'08
